# Cloud Run scheduled warmer

Keep one Cloud Run instance warm during expected usage windows
(Fri/Sat/Sun 9am–8pm America/New_York) so volunteers don't hit a
cold start. Cheaper than `min-instances=1` for a usage-window app
because the container only handles real traffic + a tiny heartbeat
poll the rest of the week.

## Cost ceiling

At a 5-minute poll every 11h × 3d/week ≈ 143 hours/month of being
warm. Cloud Run's free tier covers this comfortably:

- vCPU-seconds free: 180,000/month — we use < 400 (a poll burns ~200 ms).
- GiB-seconds free: 360,000/month — 512 MiB × 143h ≈ 264k.
- Requests: ~1,716/month from Scheduler + heartbeat traffic; free
  tier is 2M.

Net: $0/month. Holds only with Neon as the database — Cloud SQL adds
$7–9 on its own.

## Endpoints

`backend/src/volunteer_call_api/main.py` exposes:

- `GET /health` — liveness probe. Run on every deploy.
- `GET /warm` — used by Cloud Scheduler and the in-app heartbeat.
  Identical payload shape to `/health` but distinct so the
  high-frequency access pattern is easy to filter out of analytics.

The frontend layout starts a `setInterval` heartbeat at
`HEARTBEAT_MS = 4 * 60 * 1000` while the user is authenticated; the
poller below is the fallback when no one is using the app.

## One-time setup

Replace `$REGION` (e.g. `us-east4`) and `$SERVICE` (e.g.
`volunteer-call-api`) with the values from `docs/GCP_SETUP.howto`.

```bash
SERVICE_URL=$(gcloud run services describe $SERVICE \
  --region $REGION --format='value(status.url)')

# Allow unauthenticated /warm — the response leaks no data, only the
# version string. Skip if Cloud Run is already public.
gcloud run services add-iam-policy-binding $SERVICE \
  --region $REGION \
  --member allUsers \
  --role roles/run.invoker

# Cron: every 5 minutes, 9am–7:59pm America/New_York, Fri+Sat+Sun.
gcloud scheduler jobs create http rtaff-warmer \
  --location $REGION \
  --schedule="*/5 9-19 * * 5-0" \
  --time-zone="America/New_York" \
  --uri="$SERVICE_URL/warm" \
  --http-method=GET
```

To narrow the schedule later, e.g. only Saturday + Sunday morning,
edit the cron expression with `gcloud scheduler jobs update http
rtaff-warmer --schedule=...`.

## What to verify after deploying

1. **Scheduler is firing.** `gcloud scheduler jobs describe
   rtaff-warmer --location $REGION` — should show a recent
   `lastAttemptTime`.
2. **Cloud Run is staying warm during the window.** In Cloud
   Logging, filter `httpRequest.requestUrl="/warm"` — there should
   be a poll every 5 minutes during the schedule and gaps outside
   it.
3. **Cold starts only happen outside the window.** Filter
   `httpRequest.latency>"1s"` for the first request of a session
   outside Fri–Sun 9-19. A handful per week is fine; if you see
   them inside the window, widen the schedule or shorten the poll.

## When to abandon this and just use min-instances=1

If usage spreads outside Fri–Sun, or if even a one-off 800ms cold
start outside the window is unacceptable, set `min-instances=1` on
the Cloud Run service. That's $5–10/month idle and deletes this
whole knob. Keep the `/warm` endpoint either way — it's the same
heartbeat target whether driven by Scheduler or by the frontend.
